-+{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792394595018,"sessionID":"ses_eacf54832ffeFAS4YOYdKea9Bo","part":{"partID":"prt_1530b0a9a001c6Ztcq8u1mVvQa","sessionID":"ses_eacf54832ffeFAS4YOYdKea9Bo","messageID":"msg_1530afebc001pyqWuQfsVq6KV2","type":"tool","id":"call_00_yc3ngkatenw30uc7ia0dh58u","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"ls -la openspec/changes/219-rust-specs-parity/.run/results/ 2>/dev/null; echo ---; cat openspec/changes/219-rust-specs-parity/.run/results/2.md 2>/dev/null; echo ---; cat openspec/changes/219-rust-specs-parity/tasks/2.md"},"output":"total 16\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 mathias mathias 4096 Oct 19 10:20 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 7 mathias mathias 4096 Oct 19 10:03 ..\n-rw-r--r-- 1 mathias mathias 2880 Oct 19 10:01 1.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 mathias mathias 3996 Oct 19 10:20 2.md\n---\n## Changed\n\n- `crates/kernel/specs/Cargo.toml`: added a `[features]` table with\n  `parity = []` and a `[[test]]` entry for `openspec_validate_parity_corpus`\n  with `required-features = [\"parity\"]`. Nothing else changed, so\n  `sh scripts/verify-rust.sh` (default features) does not build the target; I\n  confirmed the target is absent from `cargo test -p osq-kernel-specs --no-run`\n  and present with `--features parity`.\n- `crates/kernel/specs/tests/openspec_validate_parity_corpus.rs`: two ignored\n  corpus tests. `cli_reports_a_version` locates the CLI and asserts `--version`\n  is `<n>.<n>.<n>`. `validate_matches_openspec` compares every \"OpenSpec\n  validate parity\" item and asserts the five listed differences, with the\n  `KNOWN_DIFFERENCES` table naming each label, both pass flags and its `why`.\n- `crates/kernel/specs/tests/openspec_parity_support/`: shared helpers, all\n  under the folder the task allows, so task 3 can `mod` the CLI and project\n  helpers:\n  - `cli.rs`: `OpenSpecCli::locate` (`OSQ_OPENSPEC_BIN` or\n    `node_modules/.bin/openspec`), `run` with `OPENSPEC_TELEMETRY=0` in the\n    project folder, `version` naming the path when the CLI is missing, and\n    `is_version`.\n  - `project.rs`: `Project` builds each temporary project under\n    `CARGO_TARGET_TMPDIR`, scaffolds `templates/openspec`, and sets the specs\n    and a change (writing `# Parity fixture\\n` when a change has no\n    `proposal.md`).\n  - `trees.rs`, `replay.rs`, `report.rs`: folder/name helpers, the archive\n    landing order of rust-specs \"Archive replay\", and the comparison types\n    (`Outcome`, `Comparison`, `KnownDifference`) with `assert_parity`, which\n    collects every failure before it panics.\n  - `items/`: the validate parity item collection. Living specs are validated\n    with `--specs` (one item per capability), archived changes in landing order\n    against the replay before they merge, living fixtures with\n    `<stem> --type spec`, delta fixtures as `specs/alpha/spec.md` of change\n    `parity`, and targets changes against `targets/specs/`. The Rust side runs\n    `validate_living_spec`, `validate_delta`, and\n    `validate_change` + `validate_merged_change`.\n\n## Deviated\n\n- The item collection (living, delta, targets, archive) lives in\n  `openspec_parity_support/items/` rather than in the single test file. The\n  task's scope allowed only `openspec_parity_support/**` as a new path, and the\n  250-line file budget made one file awkward. The folder is documented as\n  shared, and task 3 can declare it as `pub mod openspec_parity_support;`\n  without dead-code warnings.\n- I added guards to the main test that at least one living-spec item and one\n  archived-change item were compared, so a broken enumeration fails instead of\n  passing vacuously.\n- `is_version` and `text_of` are extra CLI helpers used by the version test and\n  the no-JSON failure path.\n\n## Missing context\n\n- The scope listed only the test file and `tests/openspec_parity_support/**`,\n  so a test-local module folder such as `tests/openspec_validate_parity/` was\n  not available, which is why the item collection sits under the support\n  folder.\n